Project origin and non-affiliation
-----------------------------------
LocaQL is an independent, community-driven local development platform
for BigQuery-compatible workflows. Its scope was informed by, and its
early planning referenced, the open-source project `goccy/bigquery-emulator`
(MIT License) as an optional upstream source of inspiration — LocaQL does
not incorporate that project's source code and is not a source fork of it.

LocaQL is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by, or otherwise
officially connected to Google LLC, Google Cloud, or BigQuery. "BigQuery"
and any related Google trademarks are the property of their respective
owner. LocaQL's user interface is an original design and does not copy
the Google Cloud Console's branding, logos, or visual identity. This
disclaimer is about corporate affiliation and branding — it does not
describe the actual open-source code LocaQL runs, documented below.

Third-party notice: GoogleSQL / ZetaSQL (Apache License 2.0)
--------------------------------------------------------------
LocaQL's query engine is not a hand-written GoogleSQL implementation.
It runs the real parser/analyzer from Google's `google/googlesql`
project (https://github.com/google/googlesql, formerly ZetaSQL) —
Copyright Google LLC, licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0
(http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0) — by way of a dependency
chain that compiles that engine to WebAssembly and transpiles the
result to pure Go: google/googlesql -> goccy/googlesql-wasm ->
goccy/wasm2go -> goccy/go-googlesql -> goccy/googlesqlite (imported
directly by LocaQL). Each of those goccy/* repositories publishes its
own wrapper/tooling code under the MIT License, but the GoogleSQL
engine logic transpiled inside them remains Apache-2.0-licensed,
Google-authored work — an MIT license on the surrounding tooling does
not relicense it.

This notice exists because, at the time it was written, none of those
intermediate `goccy/*` repositories shipped a NOTICE file carrying that
attribution forward themselves (verified by checking each repository
directly) — so LocaQL states it explicitly here instead of silently
passing the gap through. Apache License 2.0 is used under license, not
under any Google trademark or endorsement grant: LocaQL's own Apache-2.0
license is compatible with consuming Apache-2.0-licensed dependencies
(no license-conflict issue), and using Google's real, permissively
licensed open-source code here is exactly what "compatible with
selected Google BigQuery APIs" means in practice — the SQL dialect
